;; A Scheme/Racket/CL user writes a self-recursive lambda the way those
;; languages spell it. Turmeric has no named-lambda form, so this is correctly
;; rejected -- but the message used to say "parameter list must be a vector"
;; with the caret on `fact`, while a well-formed [n : int] sat one token later.
;; The reader went hunting for a bracket problem that did not exist, and the
;; diagnostic never mentioned either of the two forms that DO give recursion
;; here. Hitting this and concluding Turmeric has no recursive lambdas was a
;; reading that message invited; the help lines below are the fix.
;;
;; The happy-path counterpart is tests/fixtures/fn-recursion-letrec-and-named-let,
;; which runs both spellings this error recommends -- so the suggestion cannot
;; go stale without a fixture noticing.
(defn main [] : int
  (let [f (fn fact [n : int] : int (if (= n 0) 1 (* n (fact (- n 1)))))]
    (println (f 5))
    0))
